    12	# Design analysis for this D2 variant
    13	# -----------------------------------
    14	# a) Concrete behavior:
    15	#    The original task had exactly one gray trail, so the whole matching stage only had
    16	#    one global pair of endpoints and therefore only two destination sprites. Here there
    17	#    are two thin trails with different fixed colors: gray=5 and teal=8. Each colored
    18	#    trail is its own 8-connected component and has its own two endpoints. The solver
    19	#    must first separate path cells by color, then find the two sprites attached to each
    20	#    color-specific trail, and keep all of those destinations. Example trace:
    21	#    - old logic: a single gray trail touches only two corners -> keep those 2 sprites.
    22	#    - new logic: a gray trail touches the top pair, a teal trail touches the bottom pair
    23	#      -> keep 4 sprites total, recoloring each by the same border->center lookup rule.
    24	#    The changed stage is therefore destination selection, not color transfer itself.
    25	#
    26	# b) Complexity calibration:
    27	#    The grouping signal is explicit but not excessive: there are exactly two path colors
    28	#    and both appear in every generated sample. Each color supports two destinations, so
    29	#    the solver cannot collapse the task back to the original "pick one pair" behavior.
    30	#    The transfer rule remains one lookup hop, which keeps the task readable.
    31	#
    32	# c) Old implicit assumptions that must change:
    33	#    - Old `_detect_bordered_sprites(..., grey)` assumed exactly one forbidden path color.
    34	#      Now every sprite detector must exclude both path colors.
    35	#    - Old endpoint detection ran once on all gray cells. That silently depends on there
    36	#      being only one trail component and only one relevant color. Now endpoints must be
    37	#      found per colored component.
    38	#    - Old `dest_ids` contained exactly 2 sprites, so deleting all others was safe. Now
    39	#      deletion must preserve the union of destinations from both color groups.
    40	#    - Old generator only needed one source-destination lookup chain. The new generator
    41	#      must guarantee two distinct groups so the second path color changes the output.
    42	#
    43	# d) Placement legality:
    44	#    Four destination sprites occupy the four corners as 3x3 plus-shaped patches. Two
    45	#    colored trails live in non-overlapping center corridors (horizontal or vertical
    46	#    layout), so they never overwrite each other or the sprites. Source sprites are then
    47	#    searched from a small set of legal interior positions; this shape restriction is the
    48	#    minimal adaptation needed to fit 4 destinations + 2 paths + 2 sources on a 10x10
    49	#    board without accidental 4-connected sprite merges.
    50	#
    51	# e) Visual observability:
